WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.925
Film Courage: From what I saw in your book
on Amazon [A SCREENWRITER’S JOURNEY TO SUCCESS:

00:00:01.925 --> 00:00:02.925
Tips, tricks and tactics to survive as a working
screenwriter in Hollywood] when I was going

00:00:02.925 --> 00:00:05.250
through (before the interview) you said you
did not have relatives in the industry, you

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:10.180
had very supportive parents and you had to
break in what you call “the old-fashioned

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.610
way,” which was hard work, discipline, faith.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:18.610
And I was wondering if we can talk about the
faith part?

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:19.610
What is that exactly?

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:25.650
Mark Sanderson: Faith is if you don’t believe
in yourself why do you expect other people

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.119
to believe in you?

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:30.439
And it’s your dream.

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:33.370
Someone else will live their dream and why
shouldn’t you live your dream?

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:38.260
That was always my opinion and it’s scary
because you don’t know if you are going

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.170
to or if they will allow you to do it.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:50.079
So you have to write something that you care
about but also that somebody else sees that

00:00:50.079 --> 00:00:55.430
care in there and wants to make it, then the
business part they want to invest millions

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:59.230
of dollars in your idea as opposed to someone
else’s idea.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.180
And it’s the finding that company, finding
the timing.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:11.909
My spec sold because (my first and only spec
which was my fifth spec) but that opened the

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:17.479
door to assignment work so I haven’t sold
a spec since then because I’ve been blessed

00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:18.670
with assignment work, which is fine.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.770
I’ve written other specs, yes.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.130
But it’s exhausting to try to continue.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.880
It’s you know this one and maybe this one.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.329
It’s a much better experience when the producer
comes to me and says “Are you available?

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:33.090
We have this concept that’s greenlit, we
need writer.”

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.090
You’re like “Thank you very much.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.249
I would love the job.

00:01:36.249 --> 00:01:37.249
I’m available.”

00:01:37.249 --> 00:01:38.429
And I’ve turned down stuff as well.

00:01:38.429 --> 00:01:41.979
But the faith aspect, you always have to have
faith.

00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:49.130
Not blind faith but there are time when you’re
staring into that abyss and you go “I’m

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.780
going to take the leap, you know.”

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.189
When I worked a day job at the law firm, I
only had a day job for 2 years in my whole

00:01:56.189 --> 00:01:57.189
life.

00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:03.599
I worked other odd jobs like restaurants and
things but once I decided to take that day

00:02:03.599 --> 00:02:10.220
job because things were really slow in my
career, when I got the call from the production

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:15.010
company and they said we have this job, we
want to get back in business with you, I took

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:21.370
that leap, the void wasn’t so dark because
it was a job (it was money) but I could have

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:26.860
thought about and said “Well, I’ll stay
at the firm because it’s steady money and

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:31.549
I’ll do this other job and I can do both
right because I have?”

00:02:31.549 --> 00:02:35.300
But it wasn’t worth it for me to do that.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:40.760
The minute they said hang up the phone a two
week notice I took that leap because I just

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:41.760
need to do that.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:50.430
I have to live the life of a writer and luckily
that last X number of years I’ve been able

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.362
to do that, live the life of a writer, live
the life where I get up and that’s the only

00:02:52.362 --> 00:02:53.390
thing that I do.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:03:02.099
That’s my job and it’s the faith aspect
(like I said, as we discussed before) you

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:05.540
always have to have people who believe in
you but that only goes so far, they can all

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.060
yeah.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:14.510
That’s why I say don’t give your script
to your family members to read because unless

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:19.249
they’re executives at Warner Brothers because
there is no real point in it.

00:03:19.249 --> 00:03:20.810
Everybody is going to say “That’s the
best thing ever.”

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:24.680
“Well it kept my interest, you know?”

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.170
Great!

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.859
So everybody can rally around you and say
“Yeah!”

00:03:28.859 --> 00:03:34.180
And have the faith but it comes down to you
are living it, you know?

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.530
You’re sitting in front of the computer,
you’re trying to come up with ideas and

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:45.700
your choices that you make are directly affecting
your life financially and psychologically

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:51.170
and relationship-wise with other people so
there’s a lot of things involved in pursuing

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:52.299
a screenwriting career.

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:54.370
Not just “Oh, I’m going to write a script.”

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:59.409
There’s a bigger picture of how it affects
your life, especially when it becomes how

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:01.439
you pay your bills.

00:04:01.439 --> 00:04:03.379
So faith is an important part.

00:04:03.379 --> 00:04:09.890
But you have to, there’s always a fear still,
I mean when I get a new job I get nervous.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:14.599
I think I read that Henry Fonda would throw
up on stage before he went out to do a performance.

00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:16.709
You’re like “You’re Henry Fonda?”

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:24.389
But I think a little of that nervous not too
much fear but like that respect for me comes

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:27.919
into the craft.

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:30.020
My mom always says “Oh, you’ll know what
to do.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.150
You’re fine.”

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.210
To this day she says that.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.169
I go “I think I’m fine.

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:36.800
I’m pretty sure I’m fine?”

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.190
And then I finish it and go “Yeah, okay.”

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:44.130
But I don’t want to have that arrogance
of “I’ll work it in.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.330
This is fine.”

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:46.330
No, no, no.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:47.330
Absolutely not.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:54.690
So I have a tremendous faith in my ability
but I temper it with humility and also the

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:59.360
realities of the business and situations I
guess.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.790
Faith is (back to your question) is extremely
important.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.889
But I think faith in yourself first and foremost.

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:10.830
Don’t search outward for it.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:16.129
Like looking for validation from outside.

00:05:16.129 --> 00:05:22.430
Like I’ll write something and if an agent
likes it, then that validates me.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:27.190
Know and have faith in your abilities first
and foremost and then go out there and see

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:28.190
what happens.

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.620
Film Courage: Has gratitude always been a
part of your makeup?

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:35.310
I see that gratitude seems to be a strong
thing for you, but maybe I’m wrong?

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:36.310
Has it always been in your nature?

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:37.500
Mark Sanderson: You mean gratitude?

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.250
Film Courage: It seems like you recognize
things to be grateful for?

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:42.250
Mark Sanderson: Yes!

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:47.509
It seems like the longer you are in the business…it
always have been my makeup (yes), but the

00:05:47.509 --> 00:05:53.660
longer you get punched around and see how
things work, for myself.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:56.140
Everybody has a different story.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:06:00.080
You are very humbled and gracious.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:01.930
And then you say things like “I’m blessed.”

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:03.190
Yes, I feel like I’m blessed.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:08.379
But I’ve also done a lot of hard work and
a tremendous amount of sacrifice in my life

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:15.319
to devote for this and not for the sake of
being (you know) the wealth and the riches

00:06:15.319 --> 00:06:22.370
and the loot because I say there is no fame
and fortune in the screenwriting game.

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:29.620
I mean half of the writers in the writer’s
guild don’t work every year and those are

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.500
professional WGA writers.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:38.430
I think the odds last year were like 5,200
something out of approximately 10,000.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:43.530
So the other half are looking for jobs and
not working or the 50,000 scripts that are

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:49.300
apparently registered with the guild every
year those are just the ones registered or

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:52.350
the 70 to 100 specs a year that sell.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:57.629
And I always tell writers I don’t bring
up those odds to scare you but for you to

00:06:57.629 --> 00:07:02.659
realize what you’re up against, you wouldn’t
climb Mount Everest without the training and

00:07:02.659 --> 00:07:05.510
the help and the tools, you just go “Okay,
I’m going to go do it.”

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:11.050
Now there’s a certain courage in doing that,
but there’s also a certain stupidity at

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.610
some time.

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:16.599
And just like writing your script and being
eager and someone says “I want to read it!”

00:07:16.599 --> 00:07:23.360
And you give it to the wrong person and then
that bad note circulates, you’re finished.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:24.949
Things like that.

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:30.840
Film Courage: With this job at the law firm,
so you got this movie or you got some other

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.409
show or something, did that pan out?

00:07:34.409 --> 00:07:36.580
Mark Sanderson: It was made.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:40.460
I went to the set while I was working, that
was the hardest part where I am like “I

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.490
don’t want to go back.”

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:44.669
And then I had to go back.

00:07:44.669 --> 00:07:46.069
And I’m like deja vu.

00:07:46.069 --> 00:07:52.610
I’m back in the situation I was however
many years before with doing the catering

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:57.639
when I sold my first spec because the way
monies are doled out in the step deal just

00:07:57.639 --> 00:08:02.409
because it’s optioned or whatever doesn’t
mean you can quit your job and live in Bel

00:08:02.409 --> 00:08:03.849
Air.

00:08:03.849 --> 00:08:06.669
So there’s realities about paying your bills.

00:08:06.669 --> 00:08:11.490
Same thing when I was working at the day job
but again I was like it starts to psychologically

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:15.771
on you and say “No but I’m a writer”
And it’s like yeah but you work at a thing

00:08:15.771 --> 00:08:16.771
you know?

00:08:16.771 --> 00:08:17.771
And I’m back at the tunnel again.

00:08:17.771 --> 00:08:21.610
And there was another writer there that was
a sitcom writer and he had worked on things

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:27.450
and he too hit a short thing (a slow period).

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:31.180
And he was on his way to get back to it and
he did.

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:35.130
We all sort of quit at the same time and he
got back into writing sitcoms and I was like

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:36.460
“Yeah!

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:40.940
No looking back.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:50.940
Now again I don’t have children so that
family aspect is a whole different thing.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:57.730
So what I say is from my own opinion, from
my own experience but it sort of goes for

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:03.310
everyone with disciplines and things you have
to really keep your eye on the prize and the

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:08.371
big picture as they say because you can get
lost so easily even when you’re in it and

00:09:08.371 --> 00:09:10.950
doing it, it can get lost.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:15.950
So you always have to have that bigger plan
about where am I going with my career, what

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:17.630
am I doing?

